  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Sunday)\nIssued at 1210 PM CDT MON OCT 12 2026\n\nToday through Wednesday.\n\nBroad mid to upper-level ridging presently centered over the \nNorthwest Gulf will gradually migrate northeast to become centered\nacross Louisiana and Southern Mississippi by early in the day on \nWednesday. Toward the surface, elongated surface high pressure \ncurrently extends from across the Northern Gulf northeast into \nEastern Tennessee. Surface high pressure will retain its elongated\nstate as it becomes positioned from across the Northern Gulf \nCoast northeast across Georgia and the Carolinas through midweek. \nWinds will remain generally from the west to Northwest from 5-10 \nmph during the day with near calm winds overnight. High \ntemperatures will increase each day with the highest values west \nby Wednesday with highs from the upper 80s east to the low 90s \nacross the western counties. These readings may rival the record \nhigh temperatures provided in the Climate section of this \ndiscussion. Low temperatures will warm the most across the west \nwith readings from the upper 50s east to the low 60s west \nWednesday morning.\n\nThursday through Sunday.\n\nMid-level ridging will gradually become centered further east of \nthe area through late week, becoming centered offshore of the \nSoutheast Atlantic Coast on Friday. A closed low will dig south \nover the Western States before shearing out on Thursday over \nColorado before a more potent through dives southeast out of \nCanada across the Northern Plains by Thursday afternoon. This \nlongwave trough will swing over the Great Lakes Region on Friday \nwhile supporting a surface cold front that will swiftly move \nsoutheast across the Mid-South Region Friday with the front moving\ninto the state by Saturday morning. Limited return flow ahead of \nthe front will limit moisture recovery from the presently dry \nairmass and little in the way of upper-level support locally as \nthe brunt of the trough moves over the Northeast portion of the \ncountry will result in very low rain chances. The front is \nexpected to drop temperatures significantly with highs from the \nupper 60s far north and northeast to readings in the upper 70s \nsouth and southwest Saturday and Sunday while lows will fall into \nthe upper 40s north to the upper 50s far southeast Saturday \nmorning followed by lows in the mid 40s all but far south with \nreadings in the low 50s on Sunday morning this upcoming weekend.\n\n05",
